<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huesca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Osca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huesca is a city in Aragon in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. The Roman Osca was the base of the rebel general Sertorius, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a Muslim stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March</a>, held at times by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> and later part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, until Peter I of Aragon captured it in 1096. It then served as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a> until Zaragoza was taken in 1118.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huesca.jpg" alt="Huesca" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kent Wang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bell_tower_of_the_cathedral_of_Huesca_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
